+# Project rules
+
+The rules this project follows. Every change follows them.
+
+## Rules
+
+### I. No Runtime Dependencies (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
+The software is written in TypeScript with no runtime dependency, as the README states. `package.json` must
+never gain a `dependencies` entry. Use the Node.js standard library and the language itself. Development-only
+tooling (`devDependencies`, such as the TypeScript compiler and Node type definitions) is allowed only when a
+task needs it, and must never be imported by code that ships.
+
+### II. Strict TypeScript, ES Modules
+The package is ESM (`"type": "module"`). Code lives in `src/` and compiles with the existing `tsconfig.json`
+(ES2022, NodeNext modules, `strict` on, declarations emitted to `dist/`). Do not loosen compiler options. Avoid
+`any` and unchecked casts; model domain concepts (students, visitors, lessons, boards, sails, bookings) with
+explicit types. Relative imports use the `.js` extension that NodeNext requires.
+
+### III. Tests Come With the Behavior
+Every behavior change ships with tests, written with the built-in `node:test` runner and `node:assert`, and run
+with `npm test` (`node --test`). No third-party test framework. Test the rules of the booking desk through the
+public functions of `src/`, covering edge cases (boundaries, empty inputs, conflicts). Tests must be
+deterministic: inject the clock and any other source of randomness rather than reading them directly.
+
+### IV. Domain Rules Are Explicit
+The school's season runs April to October. Bookings, rentals and prices depend on the domain facts in the README,
+for example the difference between students and visitors who bring their own experience. Encode each domain rule
+in one place, name it, and test it. Never guess an unstated rule (opening hours, prices, capacity, experience
+levels): record it as an open question in the spec and settle it with `/speckit-clarify`. Handle dates and times
+explicitly, with no hidden dependence on the machine's time zone or current date.
+
+### V. Simplicity and Small Changes
+Do the smallest thing that meets the request (YAGNI). Make the change the request asks for, and leave unrelated
+code, files and the README alone. Prefer small pure functions and plain data structures to frameworks or
+abstractions.
+
+## Constraints
+
+- Runtime: Node.js with ES2022 features; build output goes to `dist/` (ignored by git, along with
+  `node_modules`).
+- Source goes in `src/`; tests sit beside the code they test or in a `test/` directory, named `*.test.ts` or
+  `*.test.js` so that `node --test` finds them. Because the runner executes JavaScript, the build or the runner
+  setup must make TypeScript tests runnable without adding a runtime dependency.
+- Do not commit generated output (`dist/`) or `node_modules`.
+
+## Development Workflow
+
+- Each request goes through Spec Kit: `/speckit-specify`, then `/speckit-clarify` if a question is open,
+  `/speckit-plan`, `/speckit-tasks` and `/speckit-implement`. Spec Kit keeps its work under `specs/`.
+- Plans must pass a Constitution Check against the principles above before design starts, and again after
+  design. Justify any deviation in the plan's complexity table.
+- Before a change is done, `npm test` passes and the TypeScript compiles under `strict` with no errors.
+- Keep the README accurate for anything the request changes in how the project is described, built or run.
+
+## Governance
+
+This constitution supersedes other practices. An amendment needs a written reason, a version bump (MAJOR for
+removing or redefining a principle, MINOR for adding one, PATCH for wording) and an update of any template or
+plan that it affects. Reviews and plans must check compliance with it.
+
